    "abstract": "E-commerce search systems typically optimize for clicks or rele- vance, overlooking the critical gap between browsing behavior and purchase intent. We propose Intent-Driven LLM Query Reformu- lator (IDLQR), a framework that uses reinforcement learning to optimize the customer journey such that products added to cart are ranked above those merely clicked. IDLQR employs a two-stage approach: first, a large LLM teacher (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B) generates high-quality query re- writes by reasoning over serialized user sessions including clicks, add-to-cart actions, and product metadata that are grounded in shopping intent. These rewrites are distilled into a compact stu- dent model (Qwen-0.6B) for real-time serving. The student is then fine-tuned with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) using a novel reward function that explicitly prioritizing conversion signals over engagement signals. Experiments on a large-scale proprietary dataset (95K customers, 820K sessions) show that IDLQR achieves parity with zero-shot models up to 13× larger while requiring a frac- tion of the computational resources, achieving a 7.9% MAP@1 and 8.3% MAP@3 improvement while maintaining sub-100ms latency suitable for production deployment. Our results demonstrate that task-specific distillation combined with reward-driven optimization is substantially more effective than model scaling alone",
